{"causes": ["The legendary tyranny of his predecessor, Fodbgen, created the conditions for a change in leadership among the Fir Bolg.", "The Fir Bolg's desire for stability and a more equitable social order prompted the rise of a king promising justice.", "Pre-existing Celtic mythological traditions emphasized the prosperity of the land as a direct reflection of a king's righteousness."], "effects": ["Eochaid mac Eirc established a foundational mythical paradigm for just rule and societal prosperity in early Irish lore.", "His reign codified the concept of a righteous legal system within the mythological framework of ancient Ireland.", "The idealized reign of Eochaid provided a narrative contrast that framed the subsequent arrival and conflict with the divine Tuatha Dé Danann."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "fodbgen", "sourceName": "Fodbgen", "verb": "SUCCEEDS", "targetSlug": "eochaid-mac-eirc", "targetName": "Eochaid mac Eirc", "context": "Eochaid mac Eirc overthrew and succeeded the previous Fir Bolg High King, Fodbgen, to assume leadership."}, {"sourceSlug": "fir-bolg", "sourceName": "Fir Bolg", "verb": "CANONIZES", "targetSlug": "eochaid-mac-eirc", "targetName": "Eochaid mac Eirc", "context": "The Fir Bolg people, in Irish mythology, canonized Eochaid mac Eirc as their final and most just High King."}, {"sourceSlug": "eochaid-mac-eirc", "sourceName": "Eochaid mac Eirc", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "ireland", "targetName": "Ireland", "context": "The mythical reign of Eochaid mac Eirc is entirely set within the geographical bounds of ancient Ireland, as described in the Lebor Gabála Érenn."}, {"sourceSlug": "eochaid-mac-eirc", "sourceName": "Eochaid mac Eirc", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "tuatha-de-danann", "targetName": "Tuatha Dé Danann", "context": "The peaceful reign of Eochaid mac Eirc provides a significant narrative contrast to the subsequent arrival and martial conflict with the Tuatha Dé Danann."}, {"sourceSlug": "eochaid-mac-eirc", "sourceName": "Eochaid mac Eirc", "verb": "DEFINES", "targetSlug": "fir-flatha", "targetName": "Fír Flatha", "context": "The just rule and prosperity during Eochaid mac Eirc's reign serve as a prime mythological example defining the concept of *fír flatha* or 'ruler's truth' in ancient Ireland."}], "places": [{"name": "Ireland, Europe", "role": "Primary Setting"}, {"name": "Tara, Ireland", "role": "Mythical Seat of Kingship"}, {"name": "Mag Tuired, Ireland", "role": "Future Battleground"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Eochaid mac Eirc", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "eochaid-mac-eirc"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1282596", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eochaid_mac_Eirc"], "tags": ["Monarchs & Rulers", "political-systems", "empire-and-colonialism", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Eochaid mac Eirc's mythical reign established a foundational paradigm for just rule and prosperity in early Irish lore, profoundly influencing subsequent sagas and the ideal of kingship in Celtic traditions. His story provides a crucial narrative link and moral framework within the broader Mythological Cycle.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-08-23T07:02:58Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#32622834794", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "In Irish mythology Eochaid, son of Erc, son of Rinnal, of the Fir Bolg became High King of Ireland when he overthrew Fodbgen. He was the first king to establish a system of justice in Ireland. No rain fell during his reign, only dew, and there was a harvest every year.", "newValue": "Eochaid mac Eirc, often identified as the final High King of the Fir Bolg in the mythological cycles of ancient Ireland, represents a foundational archetype of righteous sovereignty. Though his existence is purely legendary, chronicled primarily in texts like the *Lebor Gabála Érenn* (Book of Invasions), his reign is depicted as a golden age of justice and abundance, preceding the arrival of the divine Tuatha Dé Danann. His story, situated within the Classical era's oral and early written traditions of Celtic peoples, is significant for establishing an early, idealized model of kingship that profoundly influenced later Irish sagas and the cultural understanding of a legitimate ruler.

Eochaid ascended to the High Kingship by overthrowing Fodbgen, marking a transition from perceived tyranny to enlightened rule. He is credited with instituting the first comprehensive system of justice in Ireland, ensuring fair judgment for all subjects. His reign was characterized by miraculous prosperity: no rain fell, only gentle dew, yet the land produced a harvest every single year, symbolizing a period of unparalleled peace and fertility under a just king. This era of peace and plenty, however, was destined to be short-lived, as the powerful Tuatha Dé Danann were soon to challenge the Fir Bolg's dominion.

The legacy of Eochaid mac Eirc endures as a powerful mythical precedent for what constitutes a just and benevolent ruler in Irish lore. His reign established a paradigm where the king's virtue was directly reflected in the prosperity of the land, a concept known as the *fír flatha* or 'ruler's truth'. This ideal continued to resonate through centuries of Irish storytelling and political thought, shaping expectations for historical kings and providing a moral compass for governance within the rich tapestry of Celtic mythology. His narrative frames the subsequent conflict with the Tuatha Dé Danann, setting the stage for the next major mythological cycle."}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-23T07:02:58Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#32622834794", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "Classical"}, {"title": "Dynastic succession and sovereign authority traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}], "newValue": ["The legendary tyranny of his predecessor, Fodbgen, created the conditions for a change in leadership among the Fir Bolg.", "The Fir Bolg's desire for stability and a more equitable social order prompted the rise of a king promising justice.", "Pre-existing Celtic mythological traditions emphasized the prosperity of the land as a direct reflection of a king's righteousness."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-23T07:02:58Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#32622834794", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "Classical"}], "newValue": ["Eochaid mac Eirc established a foundational mythical paradigm for just rule and societal prosperity in early Irish lore.", "His reign codified the concept